WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
Good to have you on models and agents, episode 65.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.980
It's May 31st, 2026.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.820
And we're looking at what actually shipped in the last week.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.520
Six episodes from the 25th through the 31st

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.700
gave us a clear picture.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.820
Steady, practical movement on agent tooling and infrastructure

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.720
rather than headline grabbing benchmark leaps.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.080
The thread that ran through everything

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.180
was lowering the friction of getting

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.940
agents to work reliably in real environments.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.420
Whether that means starting conversations

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.840
straight from structured data, tightening the guard rails

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.300
around what they can touch, or giving them ways

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.280
to improve without waiting for the next full training run.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.360
One of the more immediate changes landed in data set.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.060
Version 1.0 alpha 30 added a keyboard slash shortcut

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.160
that surfaces a jump menu for databases, tables, and queries.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.820
Plug-in hooks now let extensions add their own entries

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.720
to that menu and its empty state.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.000
And the separate data set agent plugin

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.560
uses those hooks to drop a form directly into the interface.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.460
From inside a normal database view,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.480
you can open a fresh agent conversation

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.760
without switching tools.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.260
That matters because it removes one of the small

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.900
but persistent barriers between looking at data

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.760
and letting an agent act on it.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.560
In the agents and tool use program,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.040
this kind of integration directly attacks

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.460
the long horizon reliability problem,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:26.680
the show has been tracking.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.420
Agents become easier to reach exactly

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.000
where the data already lives.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.780
From there it's natural to ask how we make the instructions

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.340
we give agents more dependable in the first place.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.700
A new paper on skill opt treats markdown skill files

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.200
as trainable parameters.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.540
A frontier model proposes bounded edits,

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.460
add, delete, or replace,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.760
and only keeps changes that improve

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.580
a held out validation set.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.540
When the edit budget stays between four and eight proposals,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.360
skills typically settle after one to four accepted edits.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.660
Removing the budget cap though,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.140
cause performance to drop across the tasks they tested.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.020
The method gives teams a repeatable loop

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.440
for turning ad hoc instructions into validated components,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.060
another incremental step on the same reliability track.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.480
Andthropics engineering post on containing

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.140
clawed agents pushes the same idea further

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.700
into day-to-day operations.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.100
Permissions have to grow alongside capabilities

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.200
instead of staying fixed from the start.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.900
Sandbox limits are applied to file system, network,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.600
and code execution scope.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.960
And the advice is to begin narrow and expand

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.540
only after demonstrated safe behavior.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.840
That pattern feeds directly into the standardization

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.540
of agent and tool protocols we've been following.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.800
Because it turns sandboxing from a one time setup

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.020
into an ongoing discipline that scales

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.080
with what the agent can actually do.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.340
Core Weaves' new unified agentic platform

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.440
tries to close another part of the loop.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.620
It lets agents iterate on their own outputs

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.060
in self-improvement cycles without waiting

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.620
for full retraining runs.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.360
The system is aimed at production workloads

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.180
that need real-time adaptation rather than scheduled updates.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.140
So builders of long-running autonomous systems

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.080
now have tighter feedback mechanisms

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.500
they can test in deployment.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.840
That connects back to the earlier tooling stories.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.320
Once you can start an agent from data

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.340
and keep its instructions validated,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.200
the next constraint is how quickly it can learn

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.300
from what it just did.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.620
Open AI's extension of Codex computer use capabilities

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.360
to Windows machines broadens the environments

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.260
where those same agents can operate.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.500
The agent cannot perform local desktop actions

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.540
and continue tasks that were started

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.380
from the chat GPT mobile app,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.080
all while running on standard Windows hardware.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.960
Users can start, review, and steer the work remotely.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.320
Early access still shows limited reliability on edge cases

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.060
but the move removes the previous macOS

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.720
or browser-only restrictions

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.180
and gives teams another concrete place

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.920
to run autonomous coding agents.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.780
Speed improvements in retrieval also showed up this week.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.100
Perplexity open sourced a unigram tokenizer

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.340
that cuts re-ranker latency by a factor of five

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.740
against the prior hugging face baseline.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.280
For any pipeline that feeds context to agents

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.620
that latency reduction is a direct, measurable win

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.420
and how quickly relevant information can be assembled.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.680
On the model side, liquid AI released LFM 2.58 BA1B,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.100
a model with 128,000 context trained on 38 trillion tokens

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.200
and positioned for edge devices.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.620
It adds another practical option

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.060
for running capable agents on local or low power hardware.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.240
Step fun introduced a 198 billion parameter

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.240
and mixture of experts vision language model

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.700
tuned specifically for coding agents

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.180
while multiple teams demonstrated that

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.860
twin 3.6 35 BA3B can be inferred on single consumer GPUs

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.500
with strong context retention.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.860
Together these releases expand the set of places

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.640
where agent workloads can run

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.040
without depending on the largest cloud clusters.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.200
Andthropics $65 billion series H round

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.420
at a $965 billion post money valuation

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.920
led by altimeter, dragoneer, greenoaks and Sequoia

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.000
sits alongside these releases.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.920
The company reported run rate revenue

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.240
above $47 billion driven by enterprise cloud usage.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.360
And the capital is earmarked for expanded research

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.760
and inference capacity.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.080
In the Frontier models program,

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.120
this continues the pattern of capability

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.240
and price leadership trading hands

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.280
while raising questions about the next release cadence.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.780
In the AI compute and inference program,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.660
the added resources could ease supply constraints

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.160
and further bend the inference cost curve

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.100
we've been monitoring.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.060
If you haven't tried the data set jump menu

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.500
with the agent plugin yet,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.880
this week gives you a low friction place

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.120
to test starting conversations directly from structured data.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:17.620
You can also experiment with skill opt style validation loops

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.400
on your own markdown instructions

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.460
to see how quickly they stabilize

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.360
under a controlled edit budget.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.260
When you grandfile our network access

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.240
to any new tool using agent

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.720
andthropic sandboxing patterns are worth reviewing

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.200
as a concrete reference.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.280
Testing the new Windows Codec support on a local machine

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.060
lets you compare steering workflows

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.300
against the earlier Mac OS path.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.660
Keep watching for further agent framework releases

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.420
that build on the sandboxing

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.400
and continuous improvement patterns reported this week.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.880
And look for more detail on how core weaves platform

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.780
handles production feedback loops in the coming days.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.560
That's models and agents for today.

